+++ b/collab/QUESTIONS.md
@@ -1444,3 +1444,7 @@ One line per product decision made while building, newest last.
 - Product owner, 2026-10-15 (board 1.34, what is found counts as left; three riders): where Arbor is the reason that a window lies mostly out of view (another window of its app sits where Arbor puts windows out of view), Arbor fetches it, once, whether it opened or was found at a start; not while that sibling is in the Dock or hidden by its person. A floating window that went out with the view is given back by Quit, pause and Float All exactly where its person left it, however little shows: a Quit that tidies moves what it never took. What stays gets the note a hand leaves, so that no later ⌃⌥0, dock or closing pulls it in. And displays that changed while Arbor was off: the windows macOS moved count as left. Arbor moves nothing at a start that it did not itself put away.
 - Product owner, 2026-10-15 (the gate's rounds of input; read in the pull request): an event that never arrives must be judged. A round says what it posted, by kind; fewer arrivals than were posted is a `FAIL` with Arbor and the instrument's own without; a sabotage drops one. It is what macOS does behind a tap gone wrong: dropped, never late. And a kind counts as judged in a journey only if both pairs have their ten events: the round before the last Quit, with an arrangement at work, is the more telling of the two.
 - Product owner, 2026-10-15 (board 1.46; which question turns): only a trial tap. On the road where the right goes first it is refused 0 to 33 ms after the first dropped event, at about a third of a millisecond a call, and in 10,169 calls it never took longer than 81 ms to answer. The flag, "is my tap still enabled?" and both preflights never turn there, and the callback is told nothing. So the clock's question is the trial tap: "Arbor goes by the tap, not by the flag", confirmed from the other side. At that price a beat of 50 ms is two thirds of a per cent of a core: too much for all day, nothing if taps are in only while Arbor's modifiers are down (IDEAS I16).
+- (what is found counts as left, built, board 1.34, 2026-10-15; the product owner agreed by message to the two exceptions and to the way out) Fetched at a start and when a window opens is a part of what was fetched before, so nothing new moves: under half of it shows **and** no 24 points of its title bar are on any display. "Its title bar" is the window's top 24 points, less than the lowest bar measured (28), so no height needs to be known; 12 of them in height are enough to hit; of a window narrower than 24 points, its whole width. On a display means on its frame: a Dock at the side is shorter than the strip macOS keeps free for it. **Two more are fetched, because Arbor and no person is the reason they are there:** a window exactly where Arbor puts windows out of view (a run that was killed left it; with a display on either side that corner shows a whole title bar next door), and a window of which under half shows while another window of its app is out of view by Arbor's doing, this run's or a killed one's, since apps open a window beside the one they have (a hold keeps that one out instead, as before). Not while the other is in the Dock or hidden: that is its person's doing.
+  What stays gets the note a person's own hand leaves, so no later layout pulls it in and it is given back as it was: a window that is found; from the second start on, one that is found where `state.json` remembers it; and one that opens with under half showing. A window that opens hanging with **more** than half showing gets none, as before: the first layout that places floating windows fits it in, until Q20 is built. Windows that had no display at all, when one comes, go by Q20's exception: under half is reason enough.
+- (the same, on the way out) **At Quit, pause and Float All Arbor gives back what it took and touches nothing else.** The last net of a quit, "whatever the bookkeeping says, nothing is left out of view", went by "under half shows" and pulled in a window its person had parked a quarter in view, and one that was in the hand. It now fetches what still counts as out of view, what is in Arbor's own corner, and what nobody could get hold of. A floating window that went out with the view goes back exactly where its person left it, however little shows there; before, under half was fitted in.
+- (the same: **what is left**, found by a reader; no lost window) A floating window that `state.json` remembers in one place and that its person has parked in another while Arbor was not running: the start sets no frame, but Arbor still remembers the old place, and the next layout, Quit, pause or Float All takes it there (board 1.48). **One measure:** `reach`, which lets a window go on hanging at a layout, had a measure of its own (the visible frame of the window's own display, 24 by 24 points, never over the top), so that a window whose title bar is only on the display next door stayed at the start and was fitted in later, or in the very step it opened. The product owner: two measures are one too many. It now asks what the start asks. **Decided since by the product owner:** the displays changed while Arbor was not running, and macOS moved windows: what Arbor finds at a start it takes as it is, whoever put it there. They count as left, and only the measure above applies.
